DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
LOCATION:Menucha Retreat and Conference Center\, 38711 E\, Historic Colum
 bia River Hwy\, Corbett\, OR
SUMMARY:Liberatory Leadership: Embracing Grief and Possibility While Buil
 ding Beloved Community (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:This Courage to Lead® retreat is designed to help participan
 ts renew their passion\, enthusiasm\, and commitment to themselves and th
 e communities they serve\, and is open to those identifying as women who 
 are in leadership—whether formally or informally.  This four-day offeri
 ng is for those seeking to hold and respond to the complexity of our time
 \, while finding rest and rejuvenation through practicing community toget
 her. We will meet during the fullness of spring when the abundance of lig
 ht and energy invites us to remember all that brought us to this moment. 
  As individuals and as leaders\, we can expand our capacity to be fully p
 resent to ourselves and our community\, and reclaim our power to meet our
  calling in these increasingly challenging times. With courage\, compassi
 on and creativity we will share practices to hold our current realities\,
  wherever we find ourselves.We will invite the beauty of this special lan
 d above the Columbia Gorge to be a teacher and guide.Together we will…P
 ause\, breathe\, embrace silence\, and disconnect from technology and our
  demanding pace of life  Reduce isolation and build communities of care t
 hat provide support in hard times\, and act as containers for liberatory 
 innovations  Identify reflective practices that help us embrace grief and
  build hope for the future of our communities  Deepen our skills to ask o
 pen-ended questions that help us and other to uncover inner wisdom  Expan
 d capacity to be fully present to ourselves and others in ways that affir
 m and heal  Strengthen our ability to hold paradox\, moving beyond either
 -or thinking into holding opposing truths and complexity  Learn and parti
 cipate in a Clearness Committee (https://couragerenewal.org/library/the-c
 learness-committee-a-communal-approach-to-discernment/)\, designed after 
 a powerful problem-solving model from the 1600's    We are excited to hav
 e you join in retreat\, grounded in the Courage &amp\; Renewal® approach
 \, upon which Circles of Trust (http://couragerenewal.org/approach/)® is
  based\, designed to build a trustworthy space for telling our truths and
  listening to the truths of others.Retreat Details &amp\;&#160\;Registrat
 ionDate and Time: Sunday-Wednesday\, May 17-20\, 2026. The retreat will b
 egin at 4 p.m. Sunday and conclude at 11 a.m. on Wednesday.Location: Menu
 cha Retreat and Conference Center\, Corbett\, ORFacilitators: Anita Garci
 a Morales\; Dawn Montgomery\, EdD\; and Jen Wagley\, MEd\, MDivCost: Regi
 stration fees include three nights of lodging\, 9 meals (beginning Sunday
  dinner and ending Wednesday Breakfast)\, and all supplies\, snacks\, bev
 erages\, and 18 PDUs or CEUs.Single Occupancy Room: $1\,195  Shared Occup
 ancy: $895  Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735427?epid=a1a
 UY0000023cE5)Registration for this retreat will close on Friday\, May 15t
 hAccessibility:We are committed to making our events accessible to all ne
 eds and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access need
 s. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with questions.Scholarshi
 p Fund: We strive to keep our Courage retreats accessible to all who wish
  to participate. Please consider making a tax-deductible donation (https:
 //engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4dfb9f4e-7351-4d84-
 91fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Renewal%20Retreats%
 20Scholarship%20Fund) to our scholarship fund.About the FacilitatorsAnita
  Garcia Morales retired from Seattle Public Schools after 35 years of tea
 ching and learning from students\, coaching/mentoring educators\, and des
 igning and facilitating DEIB sessions for all district employees. Through
  'Racing to Equity'\, a consulting group Anita co-founded and co-owns\, s
 he facilitates anti-bias circles with community groups\, government agenc
 ies\, students\, educators and works with immigrant families who are just
  learning to navigate the complexities of their newly adopted home. Anita
  is a Courage &amp\; Renewal Facilitator\, a Positive Discipline Certifie
 d Associate\, and a Class Action Certified Trainer. The common thread tha
 t runs through all that Anita does is her focus on social justice and equ
 ity. Anita is on a journey of self-healing from the generational trauma t
 hat she\, like IBPOC across the country\, have inherited and continue to 
 experience. Anita also sees herself as a healer calling up her ancestral 
 gifts and gathers circles of folx on similar paths of healing and liberat
 ion. In this work\, IBPOC center themselves in their quest to show up in 
 the world more authentically and unapologetically.Dawn Montgomery\, EdD i
 s a change facilitator and insightful thought partner. She supports leade
 rs from a wide variety of fields\, including educators\, nonprofit execut
 ives\, and community administrators as they find their way in challenging
  times. Assisting humans in their discovery and reconnection to their dee
 pest longings has been a passion for many years. Her background in K-12 a
 nd college education as teacher\, principal\, and district leader support
  her coaching practice and team facilitation. She believes transformation
  is born when a circle of humans sit together to listen\, to speak their 
 deepest truths and to share their wildest imaginations for the world. She
  is expert at holding space for both individual leaders and teams as an e
 xecutive coach\, conflict mediator\, equity-focused professional develope
 r and a certified Courage &amp\; Renewal facilitator. She is committed to
  making a difference in the lives of individuals and their communities.Je
 n Wagley\, MEd\, M Div serves as Deputy Director of Houston in Action. Sh
 e formerly served as the founding Executive Director of Our Children Oreg
 on\, an advocacy and research organization committed to improving child w
 ell-being. She brings two decades of experience in organizational leaders
 hip\, grass-roots power building and comprehensive community development 
 and engagement. Jenifer is a facilitator with the Center for Courage &amp
 \; Renewal. Her work with the Center has been key to her sustainability a
 s a leader and has provided her the opportunity of supporting other leade
 rs across the country. Previously she served as the Deputy Director of Av
 enue Community Development Corporation in Houston\, TX. Jenifer is driven
  by a passion to create a more just\, healthy\, loving society where ever
 y person/being has what they need to thrive. Jen is mom to three boys and
  loves to hike\, coach basketball and build friendships. Jenifer holds tw
 o master's degrees from Baylor University and Truett Seminary.&#160\;New 
 workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For the late
 st on professional development related to your specific interests\, sign 
 up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
