BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T100000
LOCATION:Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School
SUMMARY:Trans Form: Grounding into Trans History\, Liberation\, and Hope 
 (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:This interactive\, in-person workshop will help participants 
 to build a clear understanding of the current political climate we are ex
 periencing\, and how we can leverage effective communication\, liberatory
  practices\, and community engagement to support trans lives in this mome
 nt.During the workshop participants will:Reflect on the history of anti-t
 rans legislation  Take a clear look at the past 10 years of laws affectin
 g transgender people\, both across the United States and here in Oregon\,
  to better understand the broader context shaping people's lives today.Pr
 actice effective communication  Learn strategies for talking about these 
 topics with clients and families in ways that are clear\, respectful\, an
 d appropriate for different ages.Explore actionable liberatory practices 
  Discuss what liberation looks like in action\, bringing together social 
 change research with examples of practice established by transgender lead
 ers.Map our communities  Create maps of our own communities and explore o
 pportunities to practice community\, leverage change\, and strengthen con
 nections with our people and our values.Following the workshop\, particip
 ants will be able to:Explain the social-political processes that have res
 ulted in increased political targeting of transgender and gender nonconfo
 rming communities  Speak to the local political landscape and current pro
 tections and challenges in Oregon for transgender communities  Identify a
 nd explain personal hope practice  Understand one to three leverage point
 s for enacting change can be utilized to support transgender communities 
 in the current political climate    This training is for anyone supportin
 g trans and gender-diverse youth—including educators\, therapists\, hea
 lth care providers\, organizers\, parents\, and caregivers—and is desig
 ned to deepen understanding of our current moment while embedding liberat
 ory\, trans-centered practices into daily life and work.  This workshop m
 ay meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Continuing Education requirement. 
 Click here for more information (http://www.oregon.gov/oblpct/Pages/CCCE.
 aspx)Event DetailsDate and Time: Saturday\, May 30\, 2026\, 9 a.m-4 p.m.L
 ocation: Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate SchoolInstructors: Carson Varnum\, M
 Ed\, and Finn Johnson\, PhD&#160\;RegistrationCommunity &amp\; Graduate S
 tudent Rate: $60 (no CEUs or PDUs)CEU/PDU Registration: $140\, includes 6
  CEUs or PDUs\, GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%Current GSEC Supervisors
 : $112\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUsRegister Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.
 net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002P22H)Workshop registration ends May 26\, 2026
   Community (no CEUs or PDUs) registration spots are currently full. Add 
 your name to the waitlist (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735424/?epid=a1aU
 Y000002P22H).  No-cost registrations available for GSEC Faculty and Staff
  are full. Email cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu) to join the waitl
 ist.Accessibility: We are committed to making our events accessible to al
 l needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access 
 needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.ed
 u)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2
 019\; registrations are for current students only and do not include cont
 inuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsCarson Varnum\, ME
 d\, (he/him) is a community builder and educator with an expertise in sex
 ual health\, sexualities\, peer education\, and community development. Ca
 rson specializes in building capacity in sex educators\, creating sexuali
 ty education and leadership development programming for adolescents\, and
  developing programs that center Queer and Trans communities. Carson work
 s at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School as TransActives's Head of Educati
 on and Outreach\, where he supports transgender\, non-binary\, and other 
 gender expansive youth by strengthening the systems that surround them. H
 e received his Masters of Education in Human Sexuality from Widener Unive
 rsity in 2012 and is currently a PhD student in Education and Leadership 
 at Pacific University. Carson is a big fan of being on mountains\, in riv
 ers\, and with friends.&#160\;Finn Johnson\, PhD (he/him) recently earned
  their doctorate in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies and Ethnic Stu
 dies at Oregon State University\, and is a law student at Lewis &amp\; Cl
 ark Law School. He holds an MA in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies 
 from Oregon State and a BA in Sexuality\, Gender\, and Queer Studies from
  Portland State University. Before law school\, Finn taught and developed
  undergraduate courses in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies at Orego
 n State\, focusing on queer and trans studies. He also publishes scholars
 hip in Critical Race Theory\, Critical Legal Studies\, and LGBTQIA+ STEM 
 Studies. His doctoral research has examined anti-transgender legislation 
 in the U.S. since 2016. Beyond research\, he has extensive experience as 
 an activist\, lobbyist\, and community educator in LGBTQIA+ and reproduct
 ive justice policy.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar 
 regularly. For the latest on professional development related to your spe
 cific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.
 net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This interac
 tive\, in-person workshop will help participants to build a clear underst
 anding of the current political climate we are experiencing\, and how we 
 can leverage effective communication\, liberatory practices\, and communi
 ty engagement to support trans lives in this moment.</p><h3 style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;"><strong>During the workshop participants will:</strong></h3
 ><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Reflect on the history of anti-tr
 ans legislation</strong></h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Take a clear
  look at the past 10 years of laws affecting transgender people\, both ac
 ross the United States and here in Oregon\, to better understand the broa
 der context shaping people's lives today.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;"><strong>Practice effective communication</strong></h4><p style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;">  Learn strategies for talking about these topics with clie
 nts and families in ways that are clear\, respectful\, and appropriate fo
 r different ages.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Explore actio
 nable liberatory practices</strong></h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  D
 iscuss what liberation looks like in action\, bringing together social ch
 ange research with examples of practice established by transgender leader
 s.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Map our communities</strong>
 </h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Create maps of our own communities a
 nd explore opportunities to practice community\, leverage change\, and st
 rengthen connections with our people and our values.</p><h4 style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;"><strong>Following the workshop\, participants will be able 
 to:</strong></h4><ul><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Explain the social-po
 litical processes that have resulted in increased political targeting of 
 transgender and gender nonconforming communities  </li><li style="line-he
 ight: 1.5\;">Speak to the local political landscape and current protectio
 ns and challenges in Oregon for transgender communities  </li><li style="
 line-height: 1.5\;">Identify and explain personal hope practice  </li><li
  style="line-height: 1.5\;">Understand one to three leverage points for e
 nacting change can be utilized to support transgender communities in the 
 current political climate  </li></ul><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This
  training is for anyone supporting trans and gender-diverse youth—inclu
 ding educators\, therapists\, health care providers\, organizers\, parent
 s\, and caregivers—and is designed to deepen understanding of our curre
 nt moment while embedding liberatory\, trans-centered practices into dail
 y life and work.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This workshop may mee
 t the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Continuing Education requirement. <a hre
 f="http://www.oregon.gov/oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx">Click here for more info
 rmation</a></p><hr /><h2><strong>Event Details</strong></h2><p style="lin
 e-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Date and Time:</strong> Saturday\, May 30\, 2026
 \, 9 a.m-4 p.m.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Location:</stron
 g> Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><s
 trong>Instructors:</strong> Carson Varnum\, MEd\, and Finn Johnson\, PhD&
 #160\;</p><h2 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Registration</strong></h
 2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Community &amp\; Graduate Student
  Rate:</strong> $60 (no CEUs or PDUs)</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><s
 trong>CEU/PDU Registration:</strong> $140\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUs\, GSE
 C Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>C
 urrent GSEC Supervisors:</strong> $112\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUs</p><p><a
  class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid
 =a1aUY000002P22H" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Register Online</a></p><
 p><em>Workshop registration ends May 26\, 2026</em></p><p>  Community (no
  CEUs or PDUs) registration spots are currently full. <a href="https://lc
 lark.tfaforms.net/4735424/?epid=a1aUY000002P22H">Add your name to the wai
 tlist</a>.</p><p>  No-cost registrations available for GSEC Faculty and S
 taff are full. Email <a href="mailto:cce@lclark.edu">cce@lclark.edu</a> t
 o join the waitlist.</p><div id="lw_widget_30f0017c" class="lw_widget lw_
 widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blurbs lw_widget_id_7119 lw_widget_accessibi
 lity_statement lw_widget_pagination"><ul class="lw_widget_results lw_widg
 et_results_blurbs"><li class="lw_item_1"><div class="lw_blurbs_body"><p c
 lass="p1" style="font-size: 15px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>Access
 ibility:</strong> We are committed to making our events accessible to all
  needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access n
 eeds. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or <a href="mailto:cce@lclark.edu">cce@l
 clark.edu</a>&#160\;with questions.<br/></em></p><p class="p1" style="fon
 t-size: 15px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>Please note:</strong> Disc
 ounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for current stude
 nts only and <strong>do not</strong> include continuing education credit 
 (CEU/PDUs)</em></p></div></li></ul></div><hr /><h2><strong>About the Inst
 ructors</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Carson Varnum\
 , MEd\,</strong> (he/him) is a community builder and educator with an exp
 ertise in sexual health\, sexualities\, peer education\, and community de
 velopment. Carson specializes in building capacity in sex educators\, cre
 ating sexuality education and leadership development programming for adol
 escents\, and developing programs that center Queer and Trans communities
 . Carson works at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School as TransActives's He
 ad of Education and Outreach\, where he supports transgender\, non-binary
 \, and other gender expansive youth by strengthening the systems that sur
 round them. He received his Masters of Education in Human Sexuality from 
 Widener University in 2012 and is currently a PhD student in Education an
 d Leadership at Pacific University. Carson is a big fan of being on mount
 ains\, in rivers\, and with friends.&#160\;</p><p style="line-height: 1.5
 \;"><strong>Finn Johnson\, PhD</strong> (he/him) recently earned their do
 ctorate in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies and Ethnic Studies at O
 regon State University\, and is a law student at Lewis &amp\; Clark Law S
 chool. He holds an MA in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies from Oreg
 on State and a BA in Sexuality\, Gender\, and Queer Studies from Portland
  State University. Before law school\, Finn taught and developed undergra
 duate courses in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies at Oregon State\,
  focusing on queer and trans studies. He also publishes scholarship in Cr
 itical Race Theory\, Critical Legal Studies\, and LGBTQIA+ STEM Studies. 
 His doctoral research has examined anti-transgender legislation in the U.
 S. since 2016. Beyond research\, he has extensive experience as an activi
 st\, lobbyist\, and community educator in LGBTQIA+ and reproductive justi
 ce policy.</p><div id="lw_widget_470b39ba" class="lw_widget lw_widget_typ
