BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T163000
LOCATION:Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School\, South Chapel and York 107
SUMMARY:Applied Suicide Intervention Skills Training (ASIST) [In-Person]
DESCRIPTION:The Applied Suicide Intervention Skills Training (ASIST) is a
 n intensive\, interactive and practice-dominated course for those who wan
 t to feel more comfortable\, confident and competent in providing an inte
 rvention with someone who is having thoughts of suicide.&#160\;This works
 hop will provide extensive opportunities for supported skills practice an
 d shared participant experiences that enrich learning.After this workshop
 \, participants will have an increased ability to:      Recognize when so
 meone may be thinking about suicide          Provide a skilled interventi
 on\, including connect the person to further support          Guide the d
 evelopment of a safety plan through a collaborative\, helping relationshi
 p          Consider how personal and community attitudes about suicide af
 fect someone's openness to seek help and the caregiver's ability to provi
 de it          Reduce suicide stigma and build confidence and capacity to
  provide suicide first aidWho should attend? The training is for anyone i
 n a position of trust\, including mental health and addictions profession
 als\, school psychologists\, school counselors\, educators\, healthcare w
 orkers\, peer support and recovery specialists\, housing specialists\, la
 w enforcement\, faith leaders\, and community volunteers.  "Prior to part
 icipating in ASIST\, I was reluctant to get involved in conversations inv
 olving suicide because I felt uneasy about how to engage effectively. I w
 alked away from this training with clear and applicable tools to enter a 
 conversation with confidence and fearlessness\, equipped to support and c
 onverse openly about suicide. It was absolutely one of the most valuable 
 trainings I have experienced in my career."  - Counselor\, Roosevelt High
  SchoolTraining credits are NASP and NASW-approved for Continuing Profess
 ional Development (CPD).Details &amp\; RegistrationThis course is current
 ly full. Add your name to the waitlist (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/47354
 24/?epid=a1aUY000002Unrl) to be notified if space becomes available.Dates
  and Time: Friday &amp\; Saturday\, June 26-27\, 2026\, 8:30 a.m.-4:30 p.
 m.&#160\;Location: Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School\, South Chapel and 
 York 107Instructors: Leslie Rodgers\, and Kathy Wilson-FeyCost and Credit
 s: $350\, includes 15 NASP-Approved CEUs or PDUs (12.75 NASW Credits). Al
 umni\, Adjuncts\, and GSEC Clinical Mental Health Supervisors save 20%.&#
 160\;Accessibility: We are committed to making our events accessible to a
 ll needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access
  needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.e
 du)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x
 2019\; registrations are for current students only and do not include con
 tinuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsLeslie Rodgers is
  a licensed clinical social worker and registered ASIST (Applied Suicide 
 Intervention Skills) trainer with over 20 years experience working with c
 hildren and adults in various therapeutic settings\, including group home
 s\, psychiatric hospitals\, and schools. Her current work has involved im
 plementing best practice procedures and providing staff development in st
 udent threat assessment\, suicide prevention\, and crisis response for sc
 hool districts. She holds a BA from the University of Texas and a Masters
  in Social Work from Portland State University.Kathy Wilson-Fey is a regi
 stered ASIST Trainer and has clinical therapy and teaching experience in 
 a variety of settings\, including community college\, community mental he
 alth\, and an international university. She has 15 years of experience as
  a mental health specialist in public schools\, where she worked with stu
 dents and families\, trained school staff on suicide intervention\, and c
 oordinated school support during crises. Kathy is fluent in Spanish and i
 s committed to equity work and cultural competence. She holds a BA from W
 hitman College and a Masters in Counseling Psychology from the University
  of Oregon.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularl
 y. For the latest on professional development related to your specific in
 terests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735
 441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  The Applied 
 Suicide Intervention Skills Training (ASIST) is an intensive\, interactiv
 e and practice-dominated course for those who want to feel more comfortab
 le\, confident and competent in providing <span style="background-color: 
 transparent\;">an intervention with someone who is having thoughts of sui
 cide.&#160\;</span></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><span class="s1">Thi
 s workshop will provide extensive opportunities for supported skills prac
 tice and shared participant experiences that enrich learning.</span></p><
 p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>After this workshop\, participants w
 ill have an increased ability to:</strong></p><ul><li><p style="line-heig
 ht: 1.5\;">      Recognize when someone may be thinking about suicide    
 </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Provide a skilled interv
 ention\, including connect the person to further support    </p></li><li>
 <p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Guide the development of a safety pla
 n through a collaborative\, helping relationship    </p></li><li><p style
 ="line-height: 1.5\;">      Consider how personal and community attitudes
  about suicide affect someone's openness to seek help and the caregiver's
  ability to provide it    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">    
   Reduce suicide stigma and build confidence and capacity to provide suic
 ide first aid<br /><br /></p></li></ul><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><str
 ong>Who should attend?</strong> The training is for anyone in a position 
 of trust\, including mental health and addictions professionals\, school 
 psychologists\, school counselors\, educators\, healthcare workers\, peer
  support and recovery specialists\, housing specialists\, law enforcement
 \, faith leaders\, and community volunteers.</p><p style="line-height: 1.
 5\;">  "<em>Prior to participating in ASIST\, I was reluctant to get invo
 lved in conversations involving suicide because I felt uneasy about how t
 o engage effectively. I walked away from this training with clear and app
 licable tools to enter a conversation with confidence and fearlessness\, 
 equipped to support and converse openly about suicide. It was absolutely 
 one of the most valuable trainings I have experienced in my career.</em>"
 </p><p>  - Counselor\, Roosevelt High School</p><p style="line-height: 1.
 5\;"><strong>Training credits are NASP and NASW-approved for Continuing P
 rofessional Development (CPD).</strong></p><hr /><h2><strong>Details &amp
 \; Registration</strong></h2><h5 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>This 
 course is currently full. <a href="https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735424/?e
 pid=a1aUY000002Unrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Add your name to the 
 waitlist</a> to be notified if space becomes available.</strong></h5><p s
 tyle="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Dates and Time:</strong> Friday &amp\; 
 Saturday\, June 26-27\, 2026\, 8:30 a.m.-4:30 p.m.&#160\;</p><p style="li
 ne-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Location:</strong> Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate 
 School\, South Chapel and York 107</p><p><strong>Instructors:</strong> Le
 slie Rodgers\, and Kathy Wilson-Fey</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><str
 ong>Cost and Credits:</strong> $350\, includes 15 NASP-Approved CEUs or P
 DUs (12.75 NASW Credits). Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and GSEC Clinical Mental He
 alth Supervisors save 20%.&#160\;</p><div id="lw_widget_30f0017c" class="
 lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blurbs lw_widget_id_7119 lw_wid
